      {
        "id": 16,
        "title": "1. Stones",
        "colour": [
          86,
          110,
          140
        ],
        "min": [
          -20.0,
          0.0
        ],
        "max": [
          160.0,
          580.0
        ],
        "note": "Start here. Cells with the Edges look draws the lines between irregular cells: one cell for each stone. Scale sets how many; Jitter how uneven."
      },
      {
        "id": 17,
        "title": "2. Shape them",
        "colour": [
          150,
          115,
          60
        ],
        "min": [
          200.0,
          0.0
        ],
        "max": [
          1040.0,
          330.0
        ],
        "note": "Levels, turned upside down, makes the cells white and the lines black: a shape (Lightest widens the gaps). Bevel rounds each stone into a dome; Random lifts some, Noise roughens."
      },
      {
        "id": 18,
        "title": "5. Finish",
        "colour": [
          90,
          130,
          70
        ],
        "min": [
          1080.0,
          0.0
        ],
        "max": [
          1700.0,
          330.0
        ],
        "note": "Mix materials by height fills the gaps with sand. Rise sets how full they are. One repeat is 1 m across."
      },
      {
        "id": 19,
        "title": "3. A colour per stone",
        "colour": [
          160,
          80,
          95
        ],
        "min": [
          200.0,
          380.0
        ],
        "max": [
          1040.0,
          690.0
        ],
        "note": "Gradient map colours Random, one grey per stone, so each stone gets its own colour. Hue and saturation darkens their edges, only where Levels (the bevel turned upside down) is white."
      },
